  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-85641",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/b79868db47bbe4ef308975cea659dc7b964ae7fcf894ee92bbb9ee8070dcaac1.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos\u2061\\theta $ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$",
